## The key conceptual correction

I would refine the “five pure tattvas are different ratios” insight slightly.

They are probably not merely five values on one axis:

[
r=\frac{\text{aham}}{\text{idam}}.
]

They appear to be states in a multidimensional configuration space:

[
T_k
===

\left(
\text{aham salience},
\text{idam salience},
\text{will},
\text{knowledge},
\text{action},
\text{explicit nonseparation}
\right).
]

That matters because Śakti is not simply the midpoint between Śiva and Sadāśiva. It is the **capacity for the entire transformation space**. Sadāśiva and Īśvara are not only reversed ratios; they may involve different dominant powers and different modes of determination. Śuddhavidyā is not a neutral 50/50 mixture but the explicit cognition that differentiation does not cancel identity—*Tantrāloka* 4.113 is a particularly important textual anchor for that. ([Wisdom Library][3])

The real geometry may be:

[
\boxed{
\text{Śiva–Śakti}
=================

\text{the space and power of articulation},
}
]

while:

[
\boxed{
\text{Sadāśiva–Īśvara–Śuddhavidyā}
==================================

\text{three stable organizations of subject–object articulation}.
}
]

That is much sharper than the familiar ladder.

